-HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
+HTTPX recognizes the same line boundaries as `str.splitlines()` and removes line
+endings by default. Use `iter_lines(keepends=True)` to preserve the original line
+endings, including `\r\n`, `\r`, `\n`, and Unicode line separators:
+
+```pycon
+>>> r = httpx.Response(200, text="first\r\nsecond\rlast")
+>>> list(r.iter_lines())
+['first', 'second', 'last']
+>>> list(r.iter_lines(keepends=True))
+['first\r\n', 'second\r', 'last']
+```
+
+This also works when streaming, with lines returned incrementally even when a
+line ending spans response chunks. The final line is returned even if it has no
+line ending.
 
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
